  "question": {
    "text": "<p>From a solid sphere of mass <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>M</mi></math> and radius <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>R</mi><mtext>,</mtext></math> a spherical portion of radius <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfenced><mfrac><mi>R</mi><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mfenced></math> is removed as shown in the figure. Taking gravitational potential <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>V</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></math> at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>r</mi><mo>=</mo><mo>&#8734;</mo><mo>,</mo></math> the potential at the centre of the cavity thus formed is (<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>G</mi><mo>=</mo></math>gravitational constant)</p><p><img src=\"assets\\qt0183_f1cc1e141ed9.png\" style=\"width: 153px; height: 150px;\" /></p>"
  },
  "options": [
    {
      "text": "<math><mfrac><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>G</mi><mi>M</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>R</mi></mrow></mfrac></math>",
